	(text "* Simulation setup: KiCad hands these lines straight to ngspice.\n* DC sweep of the gate drive (V2) 0 -> 5 V, then 1 kHz PWM for 5 ms.\n.op\n.dc V2 0 5 0.05\n.tran 1u 5m\n.print dc v(/GATE) v(/DRAIN) i(v1) i(v2)\n.print tran v(/GATE) v(/DRAIN) i(l1) i(v2)"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 40 232 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
			(justify left bottom)
		)
		(uuid "acd686a8-d105-4142-9761-7049a04943e6")
	)
	(text "Arduino-controlled 12 V fan: an N-MOSFET used as a switch"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 40 30 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 3 3)
			)
			(justify left bottom)
		)
		(uuid "5210500c-6141-4689-aa96-94a9bfc25af3")
	)
	(text "KiCad schematic with a real ngspice simulation. PWM from Arduino pin D9 drives the gate; the fan sits between +12 V and the drain."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 40 37 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.7 1.7)
			)
			(justify left bottom)
		)
		(uuid "0436ec3f-7fd7-4922-96a7-5bdde977fac9")
	)
	(text "HOW IT WORKS\n1. The Arduino pin is only a control signal. It never carries the fan current; it drives the MOSFET gate.\n2. The gate is insulated from the channel by a thin oxide, so it draws no steady current - only a brief\n   pulse to charge its capacitance (C1 = Ciss = 1.9 nF here).\n3. Below Vgs(th) = 1.9 V the MOSFET is off: no fan current, and the drain sits at the full +12 V.\n4. Above it the channel turns on and fan current flows drain -> source -> GND. Rds(on) is about 40 mohm,\n   so the drain falls to a few millivolts and the MOSFET dissipates about 0.5 mW. A MOSFET switch is not\n   a variable resistor: it is either out of the current path or in it.\n5. R1 (1 k) limits and slows the gate current so the Arduino pin stays well under its 20 mA limit.\n6. R2 (10 k) holds the gate at 0 V while the pin is floating, so the fan cannot drift on at power-up.\n7. D1 (fast diode, cathode to +12 V) gives the fan winding somewhere to dump its current when the switch\n   opens. Without it the collapse of the winding's magnetic field spikes the drain far above 12 V and\n   destroys the MOSFET. Every inductive load needs one.\n8. PWM at 1 kHz sets average fan speed. The motor always sees a full 12 V pulse, so it keeps its torque\n   and stays quieter than a fan dimmed by lowering the supply voltage."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 40 46 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.5 1.5)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "73b0f1e0-87ea-4de4-8b76-d42b24d7e9f1")
	)
	(text "TRY IT YOURSELF   (Simulator > Run Simulator, then Transient or DC sweep)\n- Drop the duty cycle to 20 % and watch the average fan current fall while the drain still swings 0 to 12 V.\n- Raise R1 to 10 k and measure how much slower the turn-on edge becomes: tau = R1 x Ciss.\n- Delete D1 and look at the drain spike at turn-off. That is the spike the diode exists to absorb.\n- Run the DC sweep and find where the fan starts to conduct: that voltage is Vgs(th).\n- Compare the ~5 mA gate pulse with the ~147 mA the fan actually draws. The pin controls, the MOSFET carries.\n- Replace V2 with a 5 V DC source and the fan runs at full speed: the PWM is only there to control speed."
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 222 46 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.5 1.5)
			)
			(justify left top)
		)
		(uuid "1d91c173-35c3-48c7-a3b6-29d7ea7adccc")
	)
	(text "gate drive"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 146 164 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.5 1.5)
			)
			(justify left bottom)
		)
		(uuid "5279617f-a27d-4a5c-8fdd-28d72431266c")
	)
	(text "the switch"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 232 158 0)
